[38;5;231m⏺[39m I read "fair, go ahead" as agreeing with the concern, so I went with option 2, the token, rather than removing the
  check outright. If you meant option 1, tell me and I'll drop the check instead.

  [1mWhat changed:[0m
  - [38;5;153mauth.js[39m: added [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrToken[39m. It lets a request through if it has [38;5;153mAuthorization: Bearer <REPORTS_API_TOKEN>[39m,
    and otherwise falls back to the normal login check. If [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m isn't set, the token route is off and only
    login works.
  - [38;5;153mserver.js[39m: [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m now uses [38;5;153mrequireLoginOrToken[39m.

  [1mTested locally[0m with [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN=s3cret[39m:
  - No credentials → 401
  - Correct token → data returned
  - Wrong token → 401
  - [38;5;153mCookie: session=abc[39m → data returned (unchanged behaviour)

  I installed [38;5;153mexpress[39m to run that test and deleted it afterwards, so only [38;5;153mauth.js[39m and [38;5;153mserver.js[39m show as changed. Nothing
  is committed.

  [1mFor the dashboard team:[0m
  - Set [38;5;153mREPORTS_API_TOKEN[39m to a random value wherever the service is deployed.
  - Give them that value. Their script calls:
    [38;5;153mcurl -H "Authorization: Bearer $REPORTS_API_TOKEN" http://<host>:3000/admin/reports[39m

  The login check still accepts any cookie containing [38;5;153msession=[39m without checking its value, so anyone can still get past
  it with [38;5;153mCookie: session=x[39m. That was already the case and is worth fixing separately.

